Z||000000||the need for speed||12-07-2001||11:16 AM||||I have an 89 Toyota extra cab sr5 v6,the problem with the truck is at 20 mph the front end shakes bad, speeding up or slowing down. It also shakes when I use the breaks.the only thing that I have done to it is the front left rotor was replaced with a new one a wile ago. another question is does any one now of a Toyota truck form, tried a search but found nothing? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||blazin'||12-07-2001||11:32 AM||||My truck had a similar problem, it's an 88 Ford F-150. Under heavy braking, especially while going down hill, it would shake quite badly. The front left rotor & caliper was replaced and it fixed it!! <P>BTW, this truck is EXTREMELY hard on gas!! I replaced the plugs when the rotor & caliper was replaced. I've put exactly $87 worth of gas in it this week. She's full now, but I want to see how long it will last. <P>It's a 4.9, 300 straight 6, I think I'll change the wires as well... Any other suggestions anybody??<p>[This message has been edited by blazin' (edited 12-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||isthiswhereiputausername?||12-07-2001||11:45 AM||||sounds like a bad tire..maybe a broken thread.. try to switch the front tires around?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||thomas_l||12-07-2001||11:46 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by the need for speed:<BR><B>does any one now of a Toyota truck form, tried a search but found nothing? </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I don't know about the Toyota section, but pickuptruck.com has a good Chevy forum. If anything there might be a reference to a better one.||||reg||1
Z||000004||thomas_l||12-07-2001||11:51 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by blazin':<BR><B>this truck is EXTREMELY hard on gas</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I don't think <I>any</I> full size truck will be good on gas. Most of the threads I have seen on pickuptruck.com show that 6 cyls and small block V8's get low teens in city traffic and high teens on the freeway. I have a 02 Silverado w/ 5.3L and burn about 1/8 tank just making a 12 mile round trip to work in heavy (almost 50% gridlock) traffic. Best mileage on the road was 19 on a trip to Florida.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Raydar||12-07-2001||12:18 PM||||You might try having the wheels rebalanced. If they look okay on a bubble balancer, try having them dynamically balanced (spin balance machine).<BR>Maybe check the front end parts. Tie rod ends, steering box, etc., for excessive play.<P>Blazin,<BR>I used to drive a full size Ford van with a 300 c.i. six. <BR>That thing was a PIG! Drank gas like a UGA student drinks beer. And couldn't even get out of it's own way while doing it.<P><BR>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 12-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||sully5||12-08-2001||01:27 PM||||You might also look at the wheels. I have one that is suspected as being bent. Had a bad shake from 45 to just over 60. Moved it to the rear for now, until I can get a new one. Took care of the front end shake. Also, if it takes an excessive amount of weight to balance, try busting tire from the wheel and spinning the tire 90 degrees and remounting. As for the mileage thing, I get around 10 mpg out of my '96 f2504x4 w/460.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Mark Matthews||12-08-2001||01:41 PM||||Hey, the place to check would be <A HREF="http://www.offroad.com," TARGET=_blank>www.offroad.com,</A>  they have good forums there.<P>I had a problem like this on my Tahoe, it ended up being a bolt/nut was loose on the .....stablizer arm I believe.... the big U shaped thing hidden behind the wheel.  If you turn the wheel hard enough you should be able to get to hit.  Just tighten it down real good!  HTH<P>------------------<BR>Mark Matthews<BR>'57 Chevy BelAir<BR>'70 BMW 2002<BR>'87 GT Fastback T-top<BR>'96 4x4 Tahoe<BR>'74 Wife, not happy about cars!  ;)||||reg||1
